  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper-level cut-off low and associated surface cyclone will\ngradually track overhead before departing to the north and east\nTuesday night. Persistent cool, cyclonic flow will generate a\nlake and upslope response such that isolated to scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms will occur today and Tuesday. Most of\nthis convection will be confined to the higher elevations of \nthe Southern Adirondacks and Southern Greens, but isolated \nshowers and storms could drift into or develop in the Upper-\nHudson, Capital, Taconic, Berkshire, and Eastern Catskill \nregions. This will be more likely on Tuesday as a surface trough\nand shortwave aloft rotate about the bases of the departing \ncyclones. Generally, rainfall from these showers and storms will\nbe light as moisture is limited. Additionally, given limited \ninstability; weak shear; and weakening lapse rates through peak \nheating hours, any thunderstorm will likely be brief and have \nlimited vertical growth. Therefore, there are no concerns for\nflash flooding or storms to become severe. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure builds back in from the west on Wednesday,\nyielding a return to regionwide dry conditions. However, the\ntranquility will not be long lasting as chances for showers and\nthunderstorms increase once again for the end of the work week.\nAnother cut-off low looks to develop early Thursday morning, \nbut this time well to our north such that a broad trough extends\nsouth into the Ohio Valley and gradually moves east into our \narea. An associated surface low then looks to send a series of\nboundaries through eastern New York and western New England \nthrough Friday, yielding potentially multiple rounds of showers\nand thunderstorms. Confidence is not yet high regarding the\nsevere weather potential given inexact timing and evolutionary\ndetails. However, given the projection of weak deep-layer shear\nand a potentially early onset of showers on Thursday morning, \nit's safe to say at this point that the overall severe threat\nThursday is low. Friday could be the better day for severe\nweather potential given increased forcing with the passage of a\ncold front and upper-level shortwave. One limiting factor is the\nweak shear portrayed in the current guidance. But, should this \nincrease, increased instability and steeper lapse rates also \nportrayed in some of the guidance could give Friday a greater \npotential for severe thunderstorms. We will continue to monitor \ntrends going forward.",
